  "query_preview": "..\nA pattern shift is on the horizon, with the dry and seasonable\ntemperatures continuing through today, then a series of low\npressure systems will bring chances of precipitation and cooler\ntemperatures. There has been just enough lingering moisture for\na few low clouds this afternoon, with clouds dissipating shortly\nafter sunset.\n\nThe first of a few low pressure system begins to impact\nSoutheast Arizona early Friday morning into Saturday afternoon.\nThis first system will progressively move from west to east\nbeginning early Friday morning for the western and central \nportions of Pima County, then working into the Tucson Metro, \nSanta Cruz, and Pinal Counties mid morning, and then spreading \nto Tucson eastward in the afternoon. This system will bring\nshowers with embedded thunderstorms, with the best chances for \nthunderstorms Friday afternoon when the best atmospheric \ninstability can be tapped into. Rainfall totals continue to be \nnot very impactful, with the current forecast having 0.01\" to \n0.30\", and locally 0.30\" to 0.60\" with any embedded \nthunderstorms. Outside of rainfall chances, surface southwest \nwinds will be elevated at 12-20 mph with gusts up to 30 mph \nFriday afternoon and again on Saturday afternoon, with winds a \ntad more westerly. \n\nOnce this system passes, there will be shortwave high pressure\nallowing for a dry period Sunday and Monday, with slightly\nincreasing temperatures to slightly below normal. Models are\nbeginning to agree on the timing of the shortwave high pressure\nallowing for the next system to come through.\n\nThe second system begins to dig down the West Coast and brings \nthe next round of precipitation begins late overnight Monday \ninto late overnight Tuesday, with some lingering showers into \nWednesday morning. The models continue to have different timing\nas to when this system weakens as a third system digs down\nbehind it late next week. Regardless, rainfall totals of \n0.01\"-0.60\" is possible with the highest amounts in the \nmountains, in Pinal County and along the International Border. \nSnow levels may come down low enough for some flurries at the \ntop of Mt Graham and White Mountains, but no accumulation is \nexpected. Temperatures begin to decrease to below normal, with \nthe coolest day on Wednesday.\n\n\nThis active pattern looks to continue into next weekend with a\nthird system, with more details being ironed out as we get\ncloser and models come into more agreement.",
